WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:01.650
Who is Shazam?

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:02.620
What makes him unique?

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:07.240
And in an era with so many superhero movies,
why did it take so long to bring him to the

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:07.820
big screen?

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:11.460
Let's take a look at The World's Mightiest
Mortal with all the stuff you need to know

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:12.840
before you see Shazam!

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:16.160
A secret identity is at the core of nearly
every superhero.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:19.640
Some, like Spider-Man, keep it secret to protect
their loved ones.

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:23.800
For Shazam, it's more because when he's not
being a superhero, he's literally a child.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:25.160
Comics can be weird.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:30.160
Unbeknownst to the DC Universe at large, Shazam
is actually Billy Batson, played in the movie

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:31.260
by Asher Angel.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:36.200
After a fateful brush with mystical forces,
Billy gains the ability to transform into

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:38.400
Shazam, "The World's Mightiest Mortal."

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.520
Decades before Stan Lee rocked the comic book
landscape with the idea that kids could relate

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:47.010
to teenage heroes like Peter Parker, Billy
Batson was the first mainstream kid protagonist

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:48.100
on the comic book scene.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:52.120
That might have been part of the reason that
his stories sold better than any other character's

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:57.650
in the 1940s, including Superman, at one point
moving over 2 million copies per month.

00:00:57.650 --> 00:00:58.480
And it makes sense.

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:02.080
What kid wouldn't want to transform into an
unstoppable magical superhero?

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:07.600
With a single magic word, Billy Batson becomes
Shazam, a lightning-powered hero played in

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:09.200
the movie by Zachary Levi.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:12.880
Exactly what that means, however, usually
depends on who's writing the comic.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:18.000
While he always turns into a pile of altruistic
super muscle with an A+ jawline, Shazam himself

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:19.400
has been a number of different things.

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:24.840
Sometimes, like in the movie, he retains Billy's
childlike mind in a superpowered adult body.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:29.520
In other iterations, he's a completely separate
entity who magically changes places with the

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:30.020
kid.

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:32.720
Either way, he's got a pretty impressive set
of super-powers.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:37.280
The magic word "SHAZAM" is actually an acrostic
listing the names of the legendary figures

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:39.350
from whom he draws his considerable powers.

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:43.579
He has the wisdom of Solomon, the strength
of Hercules, the courage of Achilles, the

00:01:43.579 --> 00:01:49.039
vaguely defined power of Zeus, the stamina
of Atlas, and the fantastic speed of Mercury,

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:51.300
which is usually used to explain how he can
fly.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:55.080
Luke had an Obi-Wan, Harry had a Dumbledore,
and Shazam has…

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:56.080
Shazam.

00:01:56.120 --> 00:02:00.400
Yes, Shazam's mentor is also called Shazam,
and yeah, it's always awkward working with

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:01.820
someone who has the same name as you.

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:06.079
Wizardly superhero exploits are sort of a
specialized field; you have to play the cards

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:06.580
you're dealt.

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:07.340
"Shazam?"

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:11.580
You're probably going to hear the word "Shazam"
a lot in this movie, so you might as well

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:12.610
get used to it now.

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:17.470
In the movie Shazam!, Billy receives the power
to turn into a hero named Shazam by saying

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:22.120
the magic word "Shazam," thanks to an ancient
wizard, who is helpfully also named Shazam.

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:26.100
The wizard is a mystical being who guards
the world from his magical cave, but you know

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:30.120
how it goes - a couple thousand years on the
job and you start feeling ready to clock out.

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:34.340
In the comics, he summons Billy to be his
new champion, granting him the aforementioned

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:38.040
abilities because Batson is a generally decent
dude with a pure heart.

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:42.920
In the original story, the wizard is immediately
and permanently squashed by a rock after handing

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:47.420
out the super-powers, but in true Obi-Wan
fashion, he occasionally returns as a wise

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:49.120
ghost to help guide Billy.

00:02:49.120 --> 00:02:53.760
In the movie, he'll be played by Djimon Hounsou,
who eagle-eyed viewers will notice has already

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:57.240
showed up in Guardians of the Galaxy, Aquaman,
and Captain Marvel.

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:00.860
That last one's a pretty interesting note,
but we'll come back to that in a second.

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:06.060
Being a superhero is a stressful job, so it's
important for a crimefighter to have a place

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:07.100
they can call their own.

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:11.020
Somewhere they can relax, talk to the ghost
of their dad who lives in a crystal, or have

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:12.980
their butler help them build a rocket car.

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:14.920
You know, the stuff we can all relate to.

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:15.920
"A lair?"

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:20.600
"Yes, like if you have something overlooking
a cliff…”

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:21.600
“overlooking some water…"

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:25.500
Shazam's secret hideout has traditionally
been the place where everything started for

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:26.940
him: the Rock of Eternity.

00:03:27.020 --> 00:03:31.700
Like so much of Shazam's story, it's changed
over the years, but generally, the facts are

00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:35.580
these: it's a magic lair, it's distinctly
rocky, and it has a rad throne.

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:39.820
In many versions, the Rock of Eternity sits
in an otherworldly realm outside of space

00:03:39.830 --> 00:03:44.490
and time, joined up to our world by mystical
means like the magic subway train that summoned

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:47.260
Billy Batson to inherit his objectively awesome
destiny.

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:52.420
It's also used as a prison for magically bound
enemies of the Shazam company of heroes, including

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:55.840
the sinful septet known as the Seven Deadly
Enemies of Man.

00:03:56.100 --> 00:04:00.080
The first time Billy Batson was summoned to
the Rock of Eternity, the Wizard Shazam had

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:05.140
some pretty unsettling furniture: a difficult-to-ignore
collection of giant statues depicting humanity's

00:04:05.140 --> 00:04:06.320
worst vices.

00:04:06.340 --> 00:04:08.380
Surprisingly enough, they weren't just statues.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:12.260
They were stone prisons built to contain the
demonic personas of each of the Seven Deadly

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:12.760
Sins.

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:17.580
Originally referred to as the Seven Deadly
Enemies of Man, they included Pride, Envy,

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:21.029
Greed, Hatred, Laziness, Selfishness, and
Injustice.

00:04:21.029 --> 00:04:25.060
Once the Comics Code lightened up a little,
they swapped the "Enemies of Man" from their

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:29.630
name with "Sins" and switched out Selfishness
and Injustice for Gluttony and the definitely

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:31.220
not code-approved Lust.

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:35.520
Needless to say, they've occasionally escaped
their stone prisons, always with the intention

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:37.740
of wreaking havoc and general bad behavior.

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:42.449
It's been confirmed by director David F. Sandberg
that they will be appearing on the big screen

00:04:42.449 --> 00:04:46.210
in Shazam!, and it's easy to suspect that
we'll see them as more than just a set of

00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:46.860
statues.

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:51.860
Since his introduction in 1941, Freddy Freeman
has been Billy's right hand man.

00:04:51.879 --> 00:04:55.639
As a comic book character with an alliterative
name, he was pretty much locked in to getting

00:04:55.639 --> 00:04:59.639
powers at some point, and sure enough, he'd
become Shazam's sidekick by the end of his

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:00.620
first appearance.

00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:05.020
Like Billy himself, Freddy was another down
on his luck orphaned kid, with a disability

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:06.900
that required him to have a crutch to walk.

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:08.139
"You're like, why so dark?

00:05:08.139 --> 00:05:10.169
You're a disabled foster kid, you've got it
all!"

00:05:10.169 --> 00:05:14.249
In the Golden Age comics, Freddy's leg and
spine were casualties of a fight between Captain

00:05:14.249 --> 00:05:16.520
Marvel and a villain named Captain Nazi.

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:21.340
Captain Marvel, feeling two heaping scoops
of superheroic guilt, shared his powers with

00:05:21.349 --> 00:05:25.259
Freddy, giving him the ability to transform
into the ever so slightly condescendingly

00:05:25.260 --> 00:05:26.960
named Captain Marvel Junior.

00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:30.400
The New 52 reboot of the character was a little
more rascally.

00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:34.120
A pickpocket with just a hint of jerk, he's
one of Billy's foster siblings.

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:38.819
The two become close, and Freddy eventually
gets a superpowered adult persona of his own.

00:05:38.819 --> 00:05:43.260
He's played in Shazam! by Jack Dylan Glazer,
familiar to many viewers thanks to his appearance

00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:45.120
as Eddie in 2017's It.

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:50.240
Doctor Thaddeus Bodog Sivana has been the
conniving arch-nemesis to Shazam since day

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:50.740
one.

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:55.740
A moral and polar opposite to the hero, he's
a small, stooped mad scientist, with his eyes

00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:58.440
perpetually obscured behind thick round glasses.

00:05:58.940 --> 00:05:59.639
He sneers.

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:00.220
He jeers.

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:01.820
He brings good guys to tears.

00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:06.400
His frequent early appearances were almost
always accompanied by a new contraption designed

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:09.820
to foil his nemesis, who he delighted in calling
the Big Red Cheese.

00:06:10.060 --> 00:06:12.740
Hey, that was a pretty sick burn back in 1941.

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:17.240
Like most comic book villains, Sivana took
a darker turn in modern stories, with a much

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:18.800
more tragic origin story.

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:23.000
After losing his family, he tries everything
from science to magic to try to bring them

00:06:23.000 --> 00:06:23.500
back.

00:06:23.660 --> 00:06:28.740
A mystical accident grants him a magic-sensing
eye, the use of which slowly decays his body.

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:33.680
Mark Strong, who plays Sivana in Shazam!,
already has a superhero movie pedigree: he

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:37.800
appeared in Kick-Ass and played Sinestro in
DC's kinda regrettable Green Lantern.

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:42.680
If Doctor Sivana is Shazam's Lex Luthor, Black
Adam is his General Zod.

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:46.000
See, the Shazam from Shazam! wasn't the wizard
Shazam's first Shazam.

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:49.560
In other words, there was another guy who
got powers from the wizard back in ancient

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:50.060
times.

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:54.599
This previous Shazam was named Adam, originally
from Egypt and sometimes from the fictional

00:06:54.599 --> 00:06:57.870
nation of Kandaq, but always from several
thousand years ago.

00:06:57.870 --> 00:07:02.820
The wizard granted Adam powers, thinking that
Adam was pure of heart, but sadly, power corrupts,

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:05.000
and super power corrupts superbly.

00:07:05.100 --> 00:07:09.520
Instead of a hero, he became Black Adam, a
supervillain overlord on his bad days and

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:12.210
a giant jerk of an antihero on the few good
ones.

00:07:12.210 --> 00:07:16.210
There haven't been any official announcements
with regard to Shazam!, but Dwayne "The Rock"

00:07:16.210 --> 00:07:19.080
Johnson has been signed on to play Black Adam
for years.

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:20.480
Could we see him in this movie?

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:24.280
Well, no one's sure yet, but you should really
know better than to leave before the credits

00:07:24.280 --> 00:07:25.660
finish if you want to find out.

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:30.980
When DC relaunched their universe in 2011
as the "New 52," all of their flagship characters

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:32.260
got a modern reimagining.

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:36.240
It wasn't the first time that Shazam had been
updated, but it was definitely the reboot

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:37.330
that changed him the most.

00:07:37.330 --> 00:07:39.160
That's how Billy Batson got a family.

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:43.990
During his reboot in Justice League, he was
taken in by Victor and Rosa Vasquez, foster

00:07:43.990 --> 00:07:45.900
parents who had grown up in the system.

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:50.599
Along for the ride were Billy's new foster
siblings, Freddy Freeman, Darla Dudley, Eugene

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:53.640
Choi, Mary Bromfield, and Pedro Pena.

00:07:53.749 --> 00:07:57.979
During his climactic fight with Black Adam,
Billy learned that he could share his abilities

00:07:57.979 --> 00:08:02.399
with his family and transformed all of his
brothers and sisters into new, lightning-cracklin'

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:04.400
superpowered versions of themselves.

00:08:04.710 --> 00:08:08.560
Victor and Rosa are set to appear in the movie,
and while Freddy's gotten the most screen

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:11.830
time in the trailers, the Shazam siblings
are along for the ride, too.

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:16.409
DC and Warner Bros. are definitely shooting
for a franchise with this movie, so don't

00:08:16.409 --> 00:08:19.680
be too surprised if you see them power up
by the time the credits roll, too.

00:08:20.060 --> 00:08:24.500
Superhero movies always throw in a few easter
eggs for die-hard fans, and Shazam's long

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:27.980
history has plenty of pretty weird characters
who might show up in the movie.

00:08:27.980 --> 00:08:31.840
Just in case they decide to toss in some oddballs,
here's who to watch out for.

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:35.860
Obviously, Freddy Freeman is showing up in
the film, and sharp-eyed fans already know

00:08:35.870 --> 00:08:40.140
that Mary Bromfield has connection to Billy
that goes back a little bit further than the

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:40.900
other kids.

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:44.580
But then you had sort of super heroes… like
Uncle Marvel.

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:48.860
He was just a chunky old dude who dressed
up in tights and hung out with a group of

00:08:48.860 --> 00:08:49.380
children.

00:08:49.460 --> 00:08:50.560
It was a different time.

00:08:50.800 --> 00:08:55.420
One of the more enduring bits of extra strength
weirdness came in the form of Tawky Tawny.

00:08:55.430 --> 00:09:00.010
When he was introduced in the '40s, Tawky
Tawny was a literal tiger who learned how

00:09:00.010 --> 00:09:04.230
to talk, wear clothes, and walk on his hind
legs because he wanted to be like people.

00:09:04.230 --> 00:09:05.600
He was grrrrrrreat!

00:09:05.790 --> 00:09:09.900
There's a more modern version of the Shazam
story that recast Tawny as a stuffed animal,

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:12.720
which would give the movie an easy way to
throw him in for fans.

00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:16.920
As for whether that stuffed tiger would come
to life and start eating bad guys, the odds

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:19.160
are pretty slim, but hey, you never know!

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:23.760
If saying your own name to transform into
a physical ideal seems a little self-centered

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:28.000
even for a superhero, take a little comfort
in the fact that Shazam didn't start out that

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:28.500
way.

00:09:28.500 --> 00:09:33.000
When he was introduced in 1939 the character
was called Captain Marvel, which technically

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:36.140
makes Shazam! the second Captain Marvel movie
of 2019.

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:38.480
"Wait, for real?"

00:09:38.780 --> 00:09:39.440
"Just say it!"

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:40.620
Here's where things get loopy.

00:09:40.900 --> 00:09:44.780
When Fawcett Comics put out their new comic
book about Captain Marvel, the Shazam one,

00:09:44.780 --> 00:09:50.860
back in 1940, DC noticed a number of similarities
to the character they'd debuted in 1938.

00:09:51.110 --> 00:09:55.180
He was a big, dark-haired dude in a cape who
could leap tall buildings and had strength

00:09:55.180 --> 00:09:56.680
that could only be called super.

00:09:56.940 --> 00:09:58.980
His costume had a familiar design to it.

00:09:59.180 --> 00:10:03.180
He was even throwing a car on the cover of
his first issue, which felt a little familiar

00:10:03.180 --> 00:10:06.710
to the company that had introduced Superman
in Action Comics #1.

00:10:06.710 --> 00:10:11.430
DC sued for copyright infringement, leading
to a messy dispute that ended with Fawcett

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:15.850
out of business, and Captain Marvel going
out of print for about two decades.

00:10:15.850 --> 00:10:20.220
In those intervening 20 years, a rival company
had appeared called Marvel Comics.

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:24.320
Since the copyright on the "Captain Marvel"
name had lapsed, these new guys had snatched

00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:28.870
it up for their own character, who - purely
by coincidence, we're sure - also changed

00:10:28.870 --> 00:10:30.740
places with a younger alter-ego.

00:10:30.860 --> 00:10:35.140
After years of using "Shazam" as the title
for comics and confusingly calling the hero

00:10:35.150 --> 00:10:39.550
"Captain Marvel" in the stories themselves,
DC would wind up officially changing the character's

00:10:39.550 --> 00:10:40.540
name to Shazam.

00:10:40.830 --> 00:10:45.080
Meanwhile, Marvel would just keep making more
and more heroes called Captain Marvel, eventually

00:10:45.080 --> 00:10:49.150
beating the Distinguished Competition to the
screen with their latest version by about

00:10:49.150 --> 00:10:49.640
a month.

00:10:49.880 --> 00:10:52.840
Seems like the speed of Mercury only takes
you so far.

00:10:53.720 --> 00:10:55.860
Check out one of our newest videos right here!

00:10:55.860 --> 00:10:59.920
Plus, even more Looper videos about your favorite
superhero movies are coming soon.

00:11:00.130 --> 00:11:04.040
Subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit the
bell so you don't miss a single one.

